Both these phrases end in "to," but this "to" is NOT the infinitive "to" — it's a preposition. So it must be followed by a GERUND (V-ing), never the base verb.

The Two Phrases

1. "Looking forward to" = expecting/anticipating something (usually positive)
2. "With a view to" = with the purpose/intention of

The Structure

Looking forward to / With a view to + V-ing (never + V1)

Examples

(a) I come here to teach.

Normal infinitive "to" — correct as is (regular purpose infinitive, different structure).

(b) ❌ I come here with a view to teach.
✅ I come here with a view to teaching.

"With a view to" needs a gerund, not the base verb — even though it looks similar to "to teach" above.

(c) I hope to meet you.

"Hope to" is a normal infinitive verb pattern — correct as is.

(d) ❌ I am looking forward to meet you.
✅ I am looking forward to meeting you.

Very commonly misused in real life — "looking forward to" needs a gerund.

(e) I am looking forward to good results.
Correct — followed by a noun here, which is also fine (since "to" is prepositional, it can take a noun or gerund, just never a base verb).


Why this happens (the trap)

Most verbs that take "to" use the infinitive "to" (to + V1): I want to go, I hope to see, I plan to visit. 
This makes people assume every "to" behaves the same way. 
But "with a view to" and "looking forward to" are exceptions — here, "to" functions as a preposition (like "to" in "I'm used to it," "I'm addicted to it"), 
and prepositions are always followed by a noun or gerund, never a base verb.
